<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="lipid-dependent phytate biosynthesis I (via Ins(1,4,5)P₃)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4541" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Information:&lt;/b&gt; Phytate (phytic acid) is the most abundant &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol phosphate (hexa&lt;i&gt;kis&lt;/i&gt;phosphate) in plant cells. Phytate serves as a storage form of &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol, phosphate and mineral nutrients for utilization during seed germination and seedling growth. It is deposited within single-membrane storage organelles referred to as protein bodies and is usually present as mixed complexes with minerals (K&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;, Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Ca&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Zn&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;) known as phytin |CITS:[11590064],[8744268]|. Up to 80% of the total seed phosphorus is stored in phytin |CITS:[15700415]|. Although phytate is found in large quantities in seeds, it is also present in other plant tissues and organs such as pollen, roots, tuber and turions (for review see |CITS:[14568069]|). The potential of phytate to form very stable complexes with mineral and proteins confers upon this molecule its notorious antinutritional properties. Humans and monogastric animals lack the hydrolytic phytases in their digestive tract. Therefore, the high concentrations of phytate in food grains and its ability to chelate mineral cations compromise mineral absorption. Undigested phytate in animal waste also contributes to eutrophication and environmental phosphorus pollution |CITS:[11590064],[11906835]|. Currently, little is known of the biosynthesis of phytate. There appears to be several routes of synthesis: a lipid-dependent route which uses Ins(1,4,5)P&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; produced from phosphatidylinositol 4,5-biphosphate hydrolysis by phospholipase C (see |FRAME:LIPASYN-PWY|), and a lipid-independent route relying on the phosphorylation of &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol or Ins(3)P. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Plants:&lt;/b&gt; In this pathway, phytate is obtained from the successive phosphorylation of Ins(1,4,5)P&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;. This latter compound is released from PtdIns(4,5)P&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; (phosphatidylinositol 4,5-biphosphate) by the action of a specific phospholipase C. The following phosphorylation steps are catalyzed by two enzymes: an Ins(1,4,5)P&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; 3-/5-/6-kinase, which in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; is encoded by two genes, &lt;i&gt;AtIPK2&amp;alpha;&lt;/i&gt; and&lt;i&gt;AtIPK2&amp;beta;&lt;/i&gt;; and an inositol polyphosphate 2-kinase, encoded by the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis AtIPK1&lt;/i&gt; gene. All of these proteins can catalyze the conversion of several inositol phosphates.</Description>
